---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { rel, SUPPORT_EMAIL, LEGAL_NAME } from '../../consts';
const description = `Privacy policy for Table Talk: Conversation Cards by ${LEGAL_NAME}.`;
---
<Base title="Privacy Policy · Table Talk: Conversation Cards" description={description} path="/privacy/table-talk">
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:720px;">
      <a class="back" href={rel('/privacy/')}>← All privacy policies</a>
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Table Talk: Conversation Cards · Effective date: September 4, 2026</p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        {LEGAL_NAME} operates Table Talk, a free conversation-card app supported by ads. There are no
        accounts, and we never see your prompts, answers, or bookmarks — those stay on your device. As of
        version 1.1.1, Table Talk also sends <strong>anonymous crash reports</strong> (Sentry) and, unless
        you turn it off in Settings, <strong>anonymous usage analytics</strong> (PostHog) — both described
        below. Neither is linked to your identity or used to track you, and neither is used for advertising.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your bookmarks and "Shuffle My Picks" deck.</li>
        <li>Your recent card/session history.</li>
        <li>Your app settings, including purchase/ads state where applicable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This information is stored locally on your device. We do not receive it. Deleting the app
        removes local app data from your device.
      </p>

      <h2>Prompts and sharing</h2>
      <p>
        All prompt text is included with the app. When you share a card, Table Talk generates an image
        on your device and hands it to the standard iOS share sheet. The destination you choose, such as
        Messages or a social app, handles that share under its own privacy practices.
      </p>

      <h2>Usage analytics (PostHog) — on by default, with a Settings opt-out</h2>
      <p>
        Table Talk sends a small set of anonymous, bucketed product events — for example, that a deck was
        opened, a card was advanced, or a bookmark was added — never a prompt, an answer, a bookmark's
        content, or anything you type. Events are tied to a random ID generated on your device, not to your
        name or any account (Table Talk has no accounts). Analytics is on by default; you can turn it off
        any time in <strong>Settings → Privacy</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Turning analytics off stops new events immediately, clears the send queue on your device, and resets your ID.</li>
        <li>Events already sent are kept for <strong>90 days</strong> as individual records and up to <strong>13 months</strong> as aggregated statistics.</li>
        <li>To have your already-sent events deleted, copy the analytics ID shown under the toggle in Settings and email it to <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> — copy it before turning analytics off, since switching off resets the ID.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Crash reporting (Sentry) — always on</h2>
      <p>
        If Table Talk crashes, it sends an anonymous crash report — the stack trace and your iOS version —
        so we can find and fix the problem. A crash report never contains a prompt, an answer, a bookmark,
        or anything else you typed or saved. There is no switch for crash reporting, because a report
        carries no user data to begin with. Crash reports are kept for <strong>30 days</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Apple Search Ads attribution</h2>
      <p>
        The first time you open Table Talk, the app asks Apple's own <strong>AdServices</strong> API whether
        your download came from an Apple Search Ads campaign, so we can tell whether that advertising is
        working. This is a first-party Apple API, not a tracking SDK: it does not use the advertising
        identifier (IDFA), does not trigger Apple's App Tracking Transparency prompt, and the raw response
        token is sent to Apple and then discarded — we only keep whether the check succeeded, not the token
        itself, and it is not linked to anything you do in the app.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising (Google AdMob)</h2>
      <p>
        The free version may show an ad as you leave a card session — never during one. Ads are served by <strong>Google AdMob</strong>,
        a third-party advertising network. Table Talk requests <strong>non-personalized</strong> ads and does not
        show Apple's tracking prompt, because it does not ask for cross-app tracking. To serve and measure
        ads, Google may still collect device information such as a device identifier, IP address, and coarse
        ad-interaction data. We do not receive, store, or link any of this to you, and it is never joined
        with the analytics described above.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>How Google uses this data is described in <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads">Google's advertising policy</a> and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>.</li>
        <li>The optional Remove Ads purchase removes ads from the app.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Used to track you</h2>
      <p>
        None of the data described on this page is used to track you across other companies' apps or
        websites, and none of it is combined with data from a data broker. This is true for analytics,
        crash reports, Apple Search Ads attribution, and advertising alike.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        We do not knowingly collect any data from anyone, including children under 13. There are no accounts,
        profiles, or in-app messaging.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy ever changes materially, the new version will be posted at this URL with an updated date
        above. Continued use of the app after a change constitutes acceptance.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Questions? Email <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
